            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Measures a constant error in a take of Shadow data that comes from calibration. In a T-pose the shoulder and wrist can hold still while the elbow turns a long way - further still for a trained, mobile dancer - and people settle into it differently, calibration to calibration. The upper-arm sensor takes whatever turn the elbow had as its zero, so for the whole take every elbow bend shows up turned by it, and the body renders with the twist piled into the shoulder.\n\nA real elbow bends forward (as SMPL's does, from its palms-down rest). So the direction a bent forearm points around the upper arm gives the turn. Only bent frames say anything, so elbow_turn needs a take, not one pose. Feed it to shadow_mag_correct's 'turn' inlet; its 'turn fade' option decides how much of it reaches a straight arm, where nothing shows it. Measure it for each take - it changes whenever the suit is calibrated.\n\nSYNTAX:\nelbow_turn\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntake in:\nShadow quaternions, (frames, 37 or 20 joints, 4), scalar first.\n\nbend from, bend to:\nOptions, in degrees (45 and 80): only frames with the elbow bent this much count. Moderate bends keep out errors that grow as the elbow closes.\n\narm low only:\nAn option (on): only frames with the upper arm more than 30 degrees below level, keeping out errors that grow as the arm rises. With fewer than 100 frames to go on, it drops this, then takes any bend past 'bend from' ('frames used' tells).\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nturn:\nDegrees, left then right. Positive: the inside of the elbow was turned up toward the ceiling.\n\nspread:\nHow much the frames used disagree, in degrees: small for a clean take.\n\nframes used:\nHow many frames each arm's turn came from.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA made-up take of both elbows bending: clean, then as it reads after a T-pose held with the elbows turned 30 degrees (left) and 15 (right).\n\nRELATED:\nshadow_mag_correct undoes the turn; elbow_hinge measures the error that changes frame to frame. The full setup on a real take is examples/shadow_mag_learning.json.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 1060,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Measures a constant error in a take of Shadow data that comes from calibration. In a T-pose the shoulder and wrist can hold still while the elbow turns a long way - further still for a trained, mobile dancer - and people settle into it differently, calibration to calibration. The upper-arm sensor takes whatever turn the elbow had as its zero, so for the whole take every elbow bend shows up turned by it, and the body renders with the twist piled into the shoulder.\n\nA real elbow bends forward (as SMPL's does, from its palms-down rest). So the direction a bent forearm points around the upper arm gives the turn. Only bent frames say anything, so elbow_turn needs a take, not one pose. Feed it to shadow_mag_correct's 'turn' inlet; its 'turn fade' option decides how much of it reaches a straight arm, where nothing shows it. Measure it for each take - it changes whenever the suit is calibrated.\n\nSYNTAX:\nelbow_turn\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntake in:\nShadow quaternions, (frames, 37 or 20 joints, 4), scalar first.\n\nbend from, bend to:\nOptions, in degrees (45 and 80): only frames with the elbow bent this much count. Moderate bends keep out errors that grow as the elbow closes.\n\narm low only:\nAn option (on): only frames with the upper arm more than 30 degrees below level, keeping out errors that grow as the arm rises. With fewer than 100 frames to go on, it drops this, then takes any bend past 'bend from' ('frames used' tells).\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nturn:\nDegrees, left then right. Positive: the inside of the elbow was turned up toward the ceiling.\n\nspread:\nHow much the frames used disagree, in degrees: small for a clean take.\n\nframes used:\nHow many frames each arm's turn came from.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA made-up take of both elbows bending: clean, then as it reads after a T-pose held with the elbows turned 30 degrees (left) and 15 (right).\n\nRELATED:\nshadow_mag_correct undoes the turn; elbow_hinge measures the error that changes frame to frame. The full setup on a real take is examples/shadow_mag_learning.json."
        },
